DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ddeevvccttll utility adjusts the state of individual devices in the ker‐
     nel's internal device hierarchy.  Each invocation of ddeevvccttll consists of a
     single command followed by command-specific arguments.  Each command op‐
     erates on a single device specified via the _d_e_v_i_c_e argument.  The _d_e_v_i_c_e
     may be specified either as the name of an existing device or as a bus-
     specific address.  More details on supported address formats can be found
     in devctl(3).

     The following commands are supported:

     aattttaacchh _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Force the kernel to re-probe the device.  If a suitable driver is
             found, it is attached to the device.

     ddeettaacchh [--ff] _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Detach the device from its current device driver.  If the --ff flag
             is specified, the device driver will be detached even if the de‐
             vice is busy.

     ddiissaabbllee [--ff] _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Disable a device.  If the device is currently attached to a de‐
             vice driver, the device driver will be detached from the device,
             but the device will retain its current name.  If the --ff flag is
             specified, the device driver will be detached even if the device
             is busy.

     eennaabbllee _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Enable a device.  The device will probe and attach if a suitable
             device driver is found.  Note that this can re-enable a device
             disabled at boot time via a loader tunable.

     ssuussppeenndd _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Suspend a device.  This may include placing the device in a re‐
             duced power state.

     rreessuummee _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Resume a suspended device to a fully working state.

     sseett ddrriivveerr [--ff] _d_e_v_i_c_e _d_r_i_v_e_r
             Force the device to use a device driver named _d_r_i_v_e_r.  If the de‐
             vice is already attached to a device driver and the --ff flag is
             specified, the device will be detached from its current device
             driver before it is attached to the new device driver.  If the
             device is already attached to a device driver and the --ff flag is
             not specified, the device will not be changed.

     cclleeaarr ddrriivveerr [--ff] _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Clear a previously-forced driver name so that the device is able
             to use any valid device driver.  After the previous name has been
             cleared, the device is reprobed so that other device drivers may
             attach to it.  This can be used to undo an earlier sseett ddrriivveerr
             command.  If the device is currently attached to a device driver
             and the --ff flag is not specified, the device will not be changed.

     rreessccaann _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Rescan a bus device checking for devices that have been added or
             removed.

     ddeelleettee [--ff] _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Delete the device from the device tree.  If the --ff flag is speci‐
             fied, the device will be deleted even if it is physically
             present.  This command should be used with care as a device that
             is deleted but present can no longer be used unless the parent
             bus device rediscovers the device via a rescan request.

     ffrreeeezzee  Freeze probe and attach processing initiated in response to driv‐
             ers being loaded.  Drivers are placed on a “frozen list” and pro‐
             cessed when a later “thaw” occurs.

     tthhaaww    Resume (thaw the freeze) probe and attach initiated in response
             to drivers being loaded.  In addition to resuming, all pending
             actions that were frozen during the freeze are performed.

     rreesseett [--dd] _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Reset the device, using bus-specific reset method.  Drivers for
             the devices being reset are suspended around the reset.  If the
             --dd option is specified, drivers are detached instead.

             Currently, resets are implemented for PCIe buses and PCI devices.
             For PCIe bus, the link is disabled and then re-trained, causing
             all children of the bus to reset.  Use --pp option of devinfo(8)
             tool to report parent bus for the device.  For PCI device, if
             Function-Level Reset is implemented by it, FLR is tried first; if
             failed or not implemented, power reset is tried.

             If you have detached or suspended a child device explicitly and
             then do a reset, the child device will end up attached.

     ggeettppaatthh _l_o_c_a_t_o_r _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Prints the full path to the _d_e_v_i_c_e using the _l_o_c_a_t_o_r method to
             get the path name.  Currently, only the “UEFI” and “FreeBSD” lo‐
             cators are implemented.  The UEFI locator constructs a path to
             the device using the rules outlines for DEVICE_PATH in the UEFI
             standard.  The FreeBSD locator constructs a path back to the root
             of the tree with the nodes separated by slashes.

